From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:01:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] daemon: reconcile wires no CNI event will ever fix

meshnet only ever acts on CNI ADD and CNI DEL. Every wire exists because some
pod's sandbox was created and is torn down because some pod's sandbox was
destroyed. That model has one hole, and it is not a rare one: a pod that
SURVIVES while its peer is recreated receives no event at all. Its half of
the wire stays exactly as it was, pointing at a peer that no longer exists,
and nothing in the system is looking at it again.

Measured 2026-09-05 on example-lab. While the CNI was failing, KubeVirt crashed
and recreated four VMs at the cdg site. The surviving pods kept stale wire
state: siteB-lan-pc01 could not reach its gateway siteB-acc-sw01 -- 172.16.14.4
answered !H at hop 1 -- while the SAME lan->core adjacency at the sfo and sea
sites answered in under 1 ms. Nothing was in CrashLoopBackOff, nothing was
Pending, and the analysis system collected the lab green over management throughout,
because the analysis system collects over management and a dead fabric certifies green.
Only something that looks at the WIRES finds this.

WHAT THIS IS NOT BUILT ON. status.skipped is HISTORICAL: it is appended to
and, apart from SkipReverse's own tuple removal, never cleared, so an entry
means "at some point this peer was not ready" and nothing more. It is not a
health signal and the reconciler does not read it.

THE RULE THAT SHAPES THE REST. A reconciler that tears down healthy wires is
far worse than the bug it fixes -- the bug costs one adjacency in one lab, a
bad reconciler costs every adjacency on a node, during a demo. So every
decision defaults to doing nothing, and a repair needs positive evidence
confirmed twice:

 1. EVIDENCE. koko.InspectVethWire and the new vxlan.Inspect are read-only
    and report `broken` only on a fact: an end ABSENT from a namespace that
    was demonstrably enterable, two ends that are demonstrably not one veth
    pair (reciprocal ifindex check), or a vxlan programmed for the wrong VNI
    or the wrong remote VTEP. Everything unproven -- a namespace that cannot
    be entered, a pair check that could not run, a non-veth or non-vxlan
    occupant, a slot owned by another wire's alias -- is `unknown` or
    `conflict` and is skipped, with the reason counted in the pass summary.
    A link present under the KubeVirt bridge-binding form of its name
    (0013's "podXXXX" -> "XXXX-nic") also stops the pass dead: the absence of
    the name we asked for proves nothing when the renamed form is there.

 2. CORROBORATION. A wire is repaired only after N consecutive passes agree,
    with the pod identity behind it (netns, container id, peer netns, peer
    VTEP) unchanged in between. This is what keeps the reconciler from
    fighting an in-flight CNI ADD or DEL: an ADD has a 90 s budget, a DEL
    45 s, and a whole tick (default 5 min) separates passes, so a wire caught
    mid-ADD settles long before it could be counted twice. A pod being
    recreated changes its identity and restarts the count from zero.

 3. IDEMPOTENT REPAIR THROUGH THE SAME PATHS A CNI ADD USES --
    koko.EnsureVethWire for a same-node veth, vxlan.CreateOrUpdate for our
    end of a cross-node wire (0014 made its removal idempotent; this builds
    on it). Both take koko's cross-process endpoint locks, so a repair that
    does race a real ADD serializes behind it. Nothing here deletes a link
    itself, and protected names (eth0, lo) are excluded before inspection.

Bounded on every axis: first pass after 90 s (MESHNET_RECONCILE_INITIAL_DELAY),
then every 5 min (MESHNET_RECONCILE_INTERVAL, 0 disables), 2 confirmations
(MESHNET_RECONCILE_CONFIRMATIONS), at most 32 repairs per pass
(MESHNET_RECONCILE_MAX_REPAIRS). Disabled outright under
INTER_NODE_LINK_TYPE=GRPC, which has its own reconciler, and it SAYS so --
a reconciler that silently does nothing is indistinguishable from a healthy
node.

Every pass logs its result, including the pass that changed nothing:
"pods=79 wires=214 healthy=214 repaired=0 failed=0 pending=0 skipped=0" is a
positive statement over a counted scope. Silence would not be.

ONE MORE THING THIS FIXES. The VNI derivation moves to wireutil.VxlanID and
plugin/meshnet.go's makeVxlanID becomes a wrapper over it. The daemon has to
derive the SAME VNI the plugin chose for a wire nobody is currently adding,
and a second copy that drifted by a byte would put the two ends of a link on
different VNIs -- up from every angle except traffic. One definition, one
behaviour; the plugin's existing tests still cover it.

Why upstream wants this: the ADD/DEL-only model is upstream's, not ours, and
the hole is in the model rather than in any one code path. A KubeVirt VM
restart is only the way we hit it; any pod restart whose peer survives does
the same thing on any substrate.

Positive controls: a table over classifyWire, an exhaustive sweep of every
combination of the boolean facts asserting `repair` is reachable ONLY with
full positive evidence (and failing if the sweep never reaches a repair at
all, so it cannot pass over an empty scope), the confirmer's four rules, and
whole passes driven through fake inspectors -- including a healthy lab, which
must change nothing and must say it changed nothing.

Executed negative controls, each of which made the suite red:
  * returning `due` on the first broken observation -> "first pass: ...
    repaired=1 ..., want the wire pending and nothing repaired"
  * treating `unknown` as broken -> "classifyWire() = repair (acting on
    inconclusive evidence ...), want skip", plus the sweep
  * removing the KubeVirt-rename guard -> "a KubeVirt-renamed wire was
    repaired"
  * removing the local-sandbox guard -> "repair without positive evidence
    {LocalNetNSUsable:false ...}"
